[{:db/id 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b046c04a-6183-5340-90ee-d461156c145c" "question-52b2f721-25f8-5813-9d4e-57bac8db769b" "question-b701c4c3-e9fc-563a-b31c-45e1ff282957" "question-5a809928-75f9-5540-949c-59cd2e66a5d3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147248 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%,$ and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 40 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 40 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{40}{10} \\\\  & =3\\times 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37153dd3-f5a5-5cb4-89d6-f95a95fd428d" "question-53cc30e1-a9ba-5d93-9a6b-7ee8476d4881" "question-d438f5d5-3cb2-537d-8c22-588564d642dd" "question-0e9d26ed-5508-5ed1-9b21-0aeebb8bd487"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147249 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%,$ and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =0.125\\times 800 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5}{100}\\times 800 \\\\  & =12.5\\times \\frac{800}{100} \\\\  & =12.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-def47a5b-79f5-55d2-884e-30c4a2ea2af7" "question-d7304750-8de2-5c9e-abd7-f379f2372291" "question-3ab23143-9ffc-5442-9bdb-5fa4de482d26" "question-98c55f63-1a03-5c99-847f-6fc5e622bbcf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147250 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1.3\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =130\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =130\\times 15 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b046c04a-6183-5340-90ee-d461156c145c" :question/id #uuid "b046c04a-6183-5340-90ee-d461156c145c" :question/math-academy-id "q-60944" :question/problem "$50%$ of $24$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes. How many students have brown eyes?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of students in Joan's class, which is $24.$ The percentage is $50%,$ and the part is the number of students that have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n50%=50÷100=0.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.5\\times 24 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n50%=\\frac{50}{100}=\\frac{50÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c89da194-a3c1-561d-9eff-8a2b3e5c9607"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c89da194-a3c1-561d-9eff-8a2b3e5c9607" :answer-field/id #uuid "c89da194-a3c1-561d-9eff-8a2b3e5c9607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a4c3a3da-c289-5d72-94a6-ce1ccba391f3" "answer-81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" "answer-b131c800-4d6f-5e41-af05-8e80beb85a4b" "answer-ac816f30-ec56-5811-83e0-1d3c797b5479" "answer-959684e0-6160-5bc3-ae20-254a6b93f357"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4c3a3da-c289-5d72-94a6-ce1ccba391f3" :answer/id #uuid "a4c3a3da-c289-5d72-94a6-ce1ccba391f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" :answer/id #uuid "81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b131c800-4d6f-5e41-af05-8e80beb85a4b" :answer/id #uuid "b131c800-4d6f-5e41-af05-8e80beb85a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac816f30-ec56-5811-83e0-1d3c797b5479" :answer/id #uuid "ac816f30-ec56-5811-83e0-1d3c797b5479" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-959684e0-6160-5bc3-ae20-254a6b93f357" :answer/id #uuid "959684e0-6160-5bc3-ae20-254a6b93f357" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-52b2f721-25f8-5813-9d4e-57bac8db769b" :question/id #uuid "52b2f721-25f8-5813-9d4e-57bac8db769b" :question/math-academy-id "q-61151" :question/problem "In Maria's class, there are $30$ children. Of this amount, $10%$ walk to school. How many children walk to school?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of children in Maria's class, which is $30.$ The percentage is $10%,$ and the part is the number of children who walk to school.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n10%=10÷100=0.1\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =0.1\\times 30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n10%=\\frac{10}{100}=\\frac{10÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0377d3b7-b2f3-5abe-89ea-7dc3a661c4b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0377d3b7-b2f3-5abe-89ea-7dc3a661c4b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0377d3b7-b2f3-5abe-89ea-7dc3a661c4b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e960dbae-8631-5175-ac7b-8cb63d220ab3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a068b8c-d7ba-58f6-aef1-67ea1b268b8b" "answer-e960dbae-8631-5175-ac7b-8cb63d220ab3" "answer-b186ff6e-a361-50ef-9788-4eabb33fa439" "answer-ad1724f0-67ed-5ad3-942a-da4b1e5cc278" "answer-9cc33901-aced-5646-ba74-8266e301a847"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a068b8c-d7ba-58f6-aef1-67ea1b268b8b" :answer/id #uuid "1a068b8c-d7ba-58f6-aef1-67ea1b268b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ children" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e960dbae-8631-5175-ac7b-8cb63d220ab3" :answer/id #uuid "e960dbae-8631-5175-ac7b-8cb63d220ab3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ children" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b186ff6e-a361-50ef-9788-4eabb33fa439" :answer/id #uuid "b186ff6e-a361-50ef-