n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =0.126\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.6%=\\frac{12.6}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.6}{100}\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =12.6\\times \\frac{19,500}{100} \\\\  & =12.6\\times 195 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00053683-f910-5b7b-b30c-4f5366d1b383"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00053683-f910-5b7b-b30c-4f5366d1b383" :answer-field/id #uuid "00053683-f910-5b7b-b30c-4f5366d1b383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" "answer-1a83b6b0-9ea3-5004-955c-fd0404996dcf" "answer-e18b17dc-80dc-58e9-846a-53998f339a61" "answer-8bd81fc7-b319-5dae-b254-133516190a44" "answer-a5893e8b-a93c-5af4-b133-8ca8b7b40265"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" :answer/id #uuid "733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$2,457$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a83b6b0-9ea3-5004-955c-fd0404996dcf" :answer/id #uuid "1a83b6b0-9ea3-5004-955c-fd0404996dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$2,547$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e18b17dc-80dc-58e9-846a-53998f339a61" :answer/id #uuid "e18b17dc-80dc-58e9-846a-53998f339a61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4,257$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bd81fc7-b319-5dae-b254-133516190a44" :answer/id #uuid "8bd81fc7-b319-5dae-b254-133516190a44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4,752$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5893e8b-a93c-5af4-b133-8ca8b7b40265" :answer/id #uuid "a5893e8b-a93c-5af4-b133-8ca8b7b40265" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$5,472$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d438f5d5-3cb2-537d-8c22-588564d642dd" :question/id #uuid "d438f5d5-3cb2-537d-8c22-588564d642dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93016" :question/problem "A machine produces $200$ light bulbs per day. Of this amount, $1.5%$ of the bulbs are defective. How many bulbs made each day are defective?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of bulbs produced, which is $200.$ The percentage is $1.5%,$ and the part is the number of defective bulbs.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n1.5%=1.5÷100=0.015\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.015\\times 200 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n1.5%=\\frac{1.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{100}\\times 200 \\\\  & =1.5\\times \\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =1.5\\times 2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce0bbe1a-ad55-5803-8aad-4b37d37084df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce0bbe1a-ad55-5803-8aad-4b37d37084df" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0bbe1a-ad55-5803-8aad-4b37d37084df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4672c57-6581-5bf1-8f3d-11582259c677" "answer-cd328c7d-a730-5ea3-b0b3-e6db72ad6ca2" "answer-3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" "answer-9c4740c7-8d67-5aa0-96ae-bf7491594956" "answer-9382fc3e-b6d4-5d3b-afcd-d5ef2ff80f70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4672c57-6581-5bf1-8f3d-11582259c677" :answer/id #uuid "f4672c57-6581-5bf1-8f3d-11582259c677" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.5$ bulbs" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd328c7d-a730-5ea3-b0b3-e6db72ad6ca2" :answer/id #uuid "cd328c7d-a730-5ea3-b0b3-e6db72ad6ca2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$15$ bulbs" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" :answer/id #uuid "3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ bulbs" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c4740c7-8d67-5aa0-96ae-bf7491594956" :answer/id #uuid "9c4740c7-8d67-5aa0-96ae-bf7491594956" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$30$ bulbs" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9382fc3e-b6d4-5d3b-afcd-d5ef2ff80f70" :answer/id #uuid "9382fc3e-b6d4-5d3b-afcd-d5ef2ff80f70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ bulbs" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147871 :question/problem "Margaret has $$1,500$ saved. Of this amount, she decides to spend $22.4%$ to buy some supplies for her farm. How much money does Margret plan to spend?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Margaret has saved, which is $$1,500.$ The percentage is $22.4%,$ and the part is the amount of money that she decides to spend.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n22.4%=22.4÷100=0.224\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =0.224\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n22.4%=\\frac{22.4}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{22.4}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =22.4\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =22.4\\times 15 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186144980 :answer-field/correct 17592186144985 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e9d26ed-5508-5ed1-9b21-0aeebb8bd487" :question/id #uuid "0e9d26ed-5508-5ed1-9b21-0aeebb8bd487" :question/math-academy-id "q-169405" :question/problem "A dough weighs $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ Of this amount, $24.5%$ represents the weight of wheat flour in the dough. How much wheat flour is in the dough?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the dough, which is $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $24.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n24.5%=24.5÷100=0.245\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =0.245\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n24.5%=\\frac{24.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =\\frac{24.5}{100}\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =24.5\\times \\frac{1,400}{100} \\\\  & =24.5\\times 14 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2be4b384-c722-53de-acff-4d66797cbf22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2be4b384-c722-53de-acff-4d66797cbf22" :answer-field/id #uuid "2be4b384-c722-53de-acff-4d66797cbf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6006ad1e-1a37-5be5-99a2-aad31c2e4c8f" "answer-a06496e3-b1f9-5236-81ae-640526c84374" "answer-9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" "answer-b0df4d7e-6c40-5596-8934-d5200922b5f4" "answer-5a93fb62-2ec9-565e-9c57-f9c082c80ac9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6006ad1e-1a37-5be5-99a2-aad31c2e4c8f" :answer/id #uuid "6006ad1e-1a37-5be5-99a2-aad31c2e4c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "315\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a06496e3-b1f9-5236-81ae-640526c84374" :answer/id #uuid "a06496e3-b1f9-5236-81ae-640526c84374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "328\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" :answer/id #uuid "9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "343\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0df4d7e-6c40-5596-8934-d5200922b5f4" :answer/id #uuid "b0df4d7e-6c40-5596-8934-d5200922b5f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "367\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a93fb62-2ec9-565e-9c57-f9c082c80ac9" :answer/id #uuid "5a93fb62-2ec9-565e-9c57-f9c082c80ac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "382\\,\\text{g}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-def47a5b-79f5-55d2-884e-30c4a2ea2af7" :question/id #uuid "def47a5b-79f5-55d2-884e-30c4a2ea2af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-93082" :question/problem "A rock band performed at two sold-out concerts. The first concert took place in a location with a $10,000$ people capacity. If the second location's capacity was $115%$ of the first location's capacity, what is the capacity of the second location?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first location, which is $10,000$ people. The percentage is $115%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second location.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n115%=115÷100=1.15\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =1.15\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n115%=\\frac{115}{100}=\\frac{115÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{23}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{20}\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =23\\times \\frac{10,000}{20} \\\\  & =23\\times 500 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ebe11f80-e67f-5114-a62e-afbd040a0f70"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebe11f80-e67f-5114-a62e-afbd040a0f70" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe11f80-e67f-5114-a62e-afbd040a0f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d3b84ac-871f-574e-a32b-2aa4c2f8047a" "answer-6b328cf7-304e-59d3-831e-6730177b6b6e" "answer-5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" "answer-384ce4c1-0ae3-5e51-b090-2d71c0255423" "answer-c824a468-35b7-55dd-b0ba-d6cf72bbd041"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d3b84ac-871f-574e-a32b-2aa4c2f8047a" :answer/id #uuid "0d3b84ac-871f-574e-a32b-2aa4c2f8047a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b328cf7-304e-59d3-831e-6730177b6b6e" :answer/id #uuid "6b328cf7-304e-59d3-831e-6730177b6b6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" :answer/id #uuid "5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-384ce4c1-0ae3-5e51-b090-2d71c0255423" :answer/id #uuid "384ce4c1-0ae3-5e51-b090-2d71c0255423" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c824a468-35b7-55dd-b0ba-d6cf72bbd041" :answer/id #uuid "c824a468-35b7-55dd-b0ba-d6cf72bbd041" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147873 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of goals Fernando scored in the first season, which is $8$ goals. The percentage is $250%,$ and the part is the number of goals Fernando scored in the second season.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250%=250÷100=2.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =2.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=\\frac{250÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186144992 :answer-field/correct 17592186144997 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-d7304750-8de2-5c9e-abd7-f379f2372291" :question/id #uuid "d7304750-8de2-5c9e-abd7-f379f2372291" :question/math-academy-id "q-169407" :question/problem "In January $2022,$ a social media account had approximately $53,000$ followers. In December of that year, the account had $120%$ the number of followers it had in January. Determine the number of followers the account had in December, $2022.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of followers the account had in January, $2022,$ which is $53,000.$ The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of followers the account had in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{53,000}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 10,600 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2ad4ce9-3fce-5321-afb5-03c2b2f94153"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2ad4ce9-3fce-5321-afb5-03c2b2f94153" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ad4ce9-3fce-5321-afb5-03c2b2f94153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d0389f3d-c421-56fc-bba0-72d413f16b66" "answer-50285ed8-6ea0-5ff3-9202-35daf33c5c53" "answer-46f4efd2-95f6-53a5-990f-05462c227746" "answer-90e6a243-58b0-5ce5-b09f-0c27c5bb5725" "answer-332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0389f3d-c421-56fc-bba0-72d413f16b66" :answer/id #uuid "d0389f3d-c421-56fc-bba0-72d413f16b66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "58,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50285ed8-6ea0-5ff3-9202-35daf33c5c53" :answer/id #uuid "50285ed8-6ea0-5ff3-9202-35daf33c5c53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "59,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46f4efd2-95f6-53a5-990f-05462c227746" :answer/id #uuid "46f4efd2-95f6-53a5-990f-05462c227746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60,600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90e6a243-58b0-5ce5-b09f-0c27c5bb5725" :answer/id #uuid "90e6a243-58b0-5ce5-b09f-0c27c5bb5725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572" :answer/id #uuid "332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "63,600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ab23143-9ffc-5442-9bdb-5fa4de482d26" :question/id #uuid "3ab23143-9ffc-5442-9bdb-5fa4de482d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-93079" :question/problem "A restaurant originally had enough capacity to serve $80$ customers. Following a renovation, the restaurant increased its capacity to $120%$ of the original. What was the capacity of the restaurant after the renovation?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the original number of customers the restaurant was able to receive, which is $80$ customers. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of customers the restaurant can receive after the renovation.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 80 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 80 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e42af57-2ee7-5552-bc66-54d7d4b4f912"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e42af57-2ee7-5552-bc66-54d7d4b4f912" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e42af57-2ee7-5552-bc66-54d7d4b4f912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4970ee8f-74a5-5f59-8b2c-a3b5b27799a1" "answer-6c91863b-dbc4-5d16-880b-03f1f4c8ba25" "answer-b24c08f0-03b4-5856-890f-04609b9bb16e" "answer-f30f15c8-5fe3-59a3-bbe3-e8a6af56f137" "answer-0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4970ee8f-74a5-5f59-8b2c-a3b5b27799a1" :answer/id #uuid "4970ee8f-74a5-5f59-8b2c-a3b5b27799a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$100$ customers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c91863b-dbc4-5d16-880b-03f1f4c8ba25" :answer/id #uuid "6c91863b-dbc4-5d16-880b-03f1f4c8ba25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$104$ customers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b24c08f0-03b4-5856-890f-04609b9bb16e" :answer/id #uuid "b24c08f0-03b4-5856-890f-04609b9bb16e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$90$ customers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f30f15c8-5fe3-59a3-bbe3-e8a6af56f137" :answer/id #uuid "f30f15c8-5fe3-59a3-bbe3-e8a6af56f137" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$92$ customers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6" :answer/id #uuid "0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$96$ customers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-98c55f63-1a03-5c99-847f-6fc5e622bbcf" :question/id #uuid "98c55f63-1a03-5c99-847f-6fc5e622bbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-61184" :question/problem "In $2018$, Elena earned a salary of $$12,000.$ The following year, she earned $110%$ of the salary received in $2018.$ What was Elena's salary in $2019?$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Elena earned in $2018$, which is $$12,000.$ The percentage is $110%,$ and the part is the amount of money she earned in $2019.$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n110%=110÷100=1.1\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =110%\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =1.1\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =13,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110%$ of $12,000$ is $13,200.$ So, Elena earned $$13,200$ in $2019.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n110%=\\frac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =110%\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =110\\times \\frac{12,000}{100} \\\\  & =110\\times 120 \\\\  & =13,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110%$ of $12,000$ is $13,200.$ So, Elena earned $$13,200$ in $2019.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b4b076c-8be9-50b1-94ed-363b1a2e52a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b4b076c-8be9-50b1-94ed-363b1a2e52a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4b076c-8be9-50b1-94ed-363b1a2e52a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93458cb2-ed4c-5d47-a882-fe5f2ed42479" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2622fd39-9743-578c-91cc-ca0821693de4" "answer-93458cb2-ed4c-5d47-a882-fe5f2ed42479" "answer-783f2ae0-2680-54b7-8c66-654710686a80" "answer-604a37eb-87cf-55a2-95bd-79746423730e" "answer-ad4867a9-bd2d-5043-a1fe-c76b24d8d7ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2622fd39-9743-578c-91cc-ca0821693de4" :answer/id #uuid "2622fd39-9743-578c-91cc-ca0821693de4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$12,800$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93458cb2-ed4c-5d47-a882-fe5f2ed42479" :answer/id #uuid "93458cb2-ed4c-5d47-a882-fe5f2ed42479" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$13,200$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783f2ae0-2680-54b7-8c66-654710686a80" :answer/id #uuid "783f2ae0-2680-54b7-8c66-654710686a80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$13,500$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-604a37eb-87cf-55a2-95bd-79746423730e" :answer/id #uuid "604a37eb-87cf-55a2-95bd-79746423730e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$14,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad4867a9-bd2d-5043-a1fe-c76b24d8d7ff" :answer/id #uuid "ad4867a9-bd2d-5043-a1fe-c76b24d8d7ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$14,400$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148699 :tutorial/content "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147655 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
